WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.410
Riddled with continuity errors and plot holes
and plagued by the notorious CGI removal of

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:10.090
a super-stache, the theatrical release of
Justice League was a huge disappointment for

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.240
many fans;
however, the Snyder Cut has finally been able

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:18.170
to fix a long list of mistakes, errors and
unanswered questions from the Joss Whedon

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:19.400
version of the movie.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:24.610
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan, and
in this video I'm explaining the top 17 DC

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:27.820
plot holes and problems fixed in Zack Snyder's
Justice League.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.410
Of course, spoilers ahead so take care.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:34.760
The theatrical release of Justice League was
vague and short on details about the Mother

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:38.640
Boxes and why they had only awoken after Superman's
death.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.440
Even worse, the Whedon reshoots ended up contradicting
the timeline established in Batman V Superman

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.740
for the experiment that Silas Stone performed
on Victor because in the theatrical release

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:53.010
Silas didn't get hold of a Mother Box until
just after Superman's death.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.780
"It was shelved till the night Superman died.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.110
Then she lit up like Christmas.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:58.150
They brought it to S.T.A.R.

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:01.880
Labs where my father recognized it as a perpetual
energy matrix."

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:06.440
This is inconsistent with BvS where Wonder
Woman saw footage of Victor's transformation

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:10.210
into Cyborg with the Mother Box before the
battle with Doomsday and subsequent death

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:11.380
of Superman.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.740
The Snyder Cut instead remains faithful to
the original timeline and establishes that

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:20.820
well before Superman died, Silas Stone found
a way to harness the energy from the Mother

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:21.820
Box.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:25.830
"Victor's father activated a Mother Box more
than a year ago when Superman was still alive.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:26.830
Didn't call out to Steppenwolf.

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:28.020
None of the boxes did.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:29.020
Not until..."

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.020
"Not until Superman died."

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:31.840
"It's like they were afraid of him?"

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:37.150
That line also explains that it was very specifically
Superman's death and the powerful, anguished

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:40.581
death cries of the Kryptonian
which we see in the opening scene of the Snyder

00:01:40.581 --> 00:01:46.140
Cut that awaken the Mother Boxes to send a
message out to Apokalips, as Diana later explains

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.100
to Bruce:
"Something woke the box my people guard.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:53.260
It called out to the dark place...
to one of Darkseid's conquerors."

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:58.030
Something introduced during Whedon's reshoots
on Justice League was the idea that Parademons

00:01:58.030 --> 00:01:59.630
can sniff out fear.

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:03.860
Batman used a criminal's fear as bait to catch
a Parademon at the beginning of the movie,

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:07.680
and Steppenwolf mentions it when he's threatening
the hostages from STAR Labs.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.569
"The stench of your fear is making my soldiers
hungry."

00:02:11.569 --> 00:02:15.720
This creates a plot hole though because at
that moment the Justice League are hiding

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.540
just outside the room where the hostages are
being held and Flash reveals that he's petrified.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:25.840
"I'm afraid of bugs and, uh, guns and obnoxiously
tall people."

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:31.459
Which begs the question, why didn't the Parademons
sense Barry Allen's fear and discover him?

00:02:31.459 --> 00:02:36.670
On top of that inconsistency, this fear concept
is used in the final battle against Steppenwolf,

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:41.120
when he becomes suddenly afraid of the Justice
League after Superman and Wonder Woman destroy

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:42.540
his axe.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:43.849
"No!

00:02:43.849 --> 00:02:45.219
This cannot be."

00:02:45.219 --> 00:02:47.420
"You recognize that smell?"

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:48.420
"Fear."

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:49.629
"Get off me!"

00:02:49.629 --> 00:02:54.079
This ends up being a rather disappointing
way for the movie's big bad to go out,

00:02:54.079 --> 00:02:59.159
especially in comparison to the final battle
in the Snyder Cut which is much more epic

00:02:59.159 --> 00:03:02.209
with Superman first slicing off one of the
villain's horns

00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:05.709
and then the team killing him and sending
his head through the boom tube to land at

00:03:05.709 --> 00:03:07.530
Darkseid's feet.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:12.209
Thankfully the whole Parademon fear subplot
and resultant plot hole has been completely

00:03:12.209 --> 00:03:16.230
removed from the Snyder Cut,
and instead the only significant mention of

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:21.129
fear is this badass moment after Steppenwolf
arrives in the Amazonian stronghold:

00:03:21.129 --> 00:03:23.819
"I will bathe in your fear."

00:03:23.819 --> 00:03:26.950
"Daughters of Themyscira, show him your fear!"

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:28.749
"We have no fear!"

00:03:28.749 --> 00:03:34.329
A cryptic moment from Bruce Wayne's Knightmare
that's never been fully explained is when

00:03:34.329 --> 00:03:38.639
Future Flash arrives to give Batman a message:
"It's Lois Lane!

00:03:38.639 --> 00:03:40.059
She's the key!"

00:03:40.059 --> 00:03:44.569
In the Whedon reshoots, it seemed this was
partially explained when Batman arranged with

00:03:44.569 --> 00:03:49.250
Alfred to bring in Lois Lane as a back-up
in case Superman's resurrection went wrong:

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:50.779
"Diana was right about the risk.

00:03:50.779 --> 00:03:53.439
If the plan goes south, we're gonna bring
in the big guns."

00:03:53.439 --> 00:03:57.769
Although in both versions of Justice League,
it's Lois who arrives just in time to calm

00:03:57.769 --> 00:04:02.730
Superman down, in the Snyder Cut, this has
nothing to do with Batman, Lois is simply

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:06.439
making her final visit to Superman's memorial
by herself.

00:04:06.439 --> 00:04:11.040
In fact, Snyder has said that the Flash's
warning from the future about Lois Lane is

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.499
referring to something much bigger and several
scenes in the Snyder Cut elaborate on what

00:04:15.499 --> 00:04:19.410
he's hinting at:
"Barry Allen was right here and he said to

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:20.940
me,
'Lois Lane is the key.'

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:22.019
"She is...
to Superman."

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:24.950
"I think it's something more.

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:25.950
Something darker."

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:30.450
We know from Cyborg's premonition that Lois
will die and the epilogue scene between Batman

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:35.530
and Joker also reveals that Batman was somehow
at fault for her death, which led Superman

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:37.800
to go to the dark side.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:42.870
"You, need me to help you undo this world
you created by letting her die."

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:47.650
The Snyder Cut also includes an interesting
additional scene with Lois where we see she

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:51.900
has a pregnancy test in her bedside drawer,
which she then picks up and goes to use in

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:53.120
the bathroom.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.730
Following on with the baby theme, there's
a notable difference in the Snyder Cut in

00:04:56.730 --> 00:05:01.270
the final scene at the Clark family homestead
where Lois is carrying a baby basket into

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:05.190
the house rather than the removal box seen
in the Whedon cut.

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:09.620
Snyder in fact has said that Lois's pregnancy
is something the studio had asked to be removed

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:13.070
before he stopped work on the movie in 2017.

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:17.470
A debatable plot hole that some fans have
noticed in the Whedon cut of the Gotham Harbour

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:20.251
tunnels scene
is that it shouldn't be possible for the Flash

00:05:20.251 --> 00:05:24.690
to move people around safely at super-speed
when they aren't wearing a protective suit

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:25.690
like him.

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:30.920
The Snyder Cut resolves this issue specifically
by not having Flash move anyone around in

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:33.650
the tunnels
and instead he simply zips back and forth

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:38.520
showing people where to go and later on he
uses his super-speed to rapidly move a stack

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:43.110
of rubble that's about to fall onto the hostages
when they get out of the tunnels.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:47.050
The dangers of the Speed Force are also clearly
shown in the Snyder Cut scene where Barry

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:51.340
Allen's shoes disintegrate as he turns in
them at super-speed when he goes to rescue

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:52.490
Iris West.

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:57.190
It goes without saying that Snyder's Justice
League restores Henry Cavill's stacheless

00:05:57.190 --> 00:06:00.530
Superman scenes,
doing away with the dreadful opening sequence

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:04.650
from the Whedon cut where the Avengers director
seemed so desperate to be acknowledged for

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:07.360
his hard work on the film that he included
this sign.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:12.000
But more importantly fans finally got to see
Superman in his iconic black suit from the

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.630
Death of Superman comic book storyline, which
had been teased before the theatrical release

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:17.630
but was never delivered.

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:23.140
The Snyder Cut also connects to and elaborates
properly on the Knightmare scene from Batman

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:27.710
V Superman, part of a much larger storyline
and something which was essentially ignored

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:31.830
in the theatrical cut of Justice League,
leaving fans wondering what the point of it

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:32.830
had been.

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:37.200
The Snyder Cut on the other hand begins to
answer some of our questions about that enigmatic

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:38.200
dream from BvS.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:43.140
The first revelation comes in the premonition
of the future that Cyborg sees just before

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:46.140
Superman is resurrected in the Kryptonian
ship.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:50.520
In that vision, we see a dark future of a
dead Wonder Woman with Darkseid arriving to

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:54.121
conquer Themyscira;
the death of Aquaman at the hands of the Dark

00:06:54.121 --> 00:06:56.930
God;
and a grieving Superman holding the charred

00:06:56.930 --> 00:07:01.889
remains of Lois Lane as he's about to be brainwashed
and taken over by Darkseid.

00:07:01.889 --> 00:07:07.490
The Snyder Cut's epilogue also continues Bruce
Wayne's dreams of a post-apocalyptic future

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:12.420
as we see the Dark Knight forced to work with
his enemies Deathstroke and the Joker.

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:16.639
This scene is also interesting because for
many fans it's a marked improvement on Jared

00:07:16.639 --> 00:07:19.330
Leto's Joker that we saw in Suicide Squad.

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:23.420
The tattoos on his head which were generally
disliked have gone or are at least covered

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:25.760
up,
and this scene now confirms his involvement

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:30.200
in the death of Robin, which was alluded to
in Batman V Superman via the graffitied costume

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:31.200
in the Batcave.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:34.260
"Why you sent the Boy Wonder to do a man's
job?"

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:38.760
This scene, which Snyder added in reshoots
specifically for this Cut, is also a nod to

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:41.330
his idea for a Batman and Joker film which
would explore the death of Robin, something

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:44.290
the director has admitted is unlikely to happen
now.

00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:49.060
A character who got completely short-changed
in the theatrical version of Justice League

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:50.060
was Cyborg.

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:54.251
It seems he was the main character who suffered
from the crazy studio mandate that the movie

00:07:54.251 --> 00:07:59.020
had to be a maximum of two hours long,
which seems especially ridiculous given that

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:03.610
in the Whedon cut screen time was given over
to adding a random Russian family in order

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:07.639
to supposedly make the conflict more relatable
to ordinary people.

00:08:07.639 --> 00:08:11.600
In the theatrical version,
Cyborg wasn't an especially interesting character

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:16.810
and his backstory was extremely underdeveloped
with just a few slight references to the accident

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:20.620
in which he lost his mother and the reason
that his father experimented on him.

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:25.060
In fact, Ray Fisher has even said that almost
every single scene of him in the theatrical

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:27.340
cut was from the Whedon reshoots.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:32.131
The Snyder Cut thankfully revolutionises our
view of Cyborg, putting him as Zack Snyder

00:08:32.131 --> 00:08:34.620
himself has said, at the heart of the story.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:38.789
We get to see Victor Stone's kindness, his
troubled relationship with his father,

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:43.849
him learning to use his powers and becoming
a crucial part of the team from fixing Batman's

00:08:43.849 --> 00:08:49.050
Flying Fox to confronting his own insecurities
in order to defeat the Mother Boxes.

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:53.810
On top of that, watching his own father sacrifice
himself in order to place a tracker on the

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:58.720
third Mother Box is a particularly emotional
moment and adds another layer to the character,

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:03.459
in sharp contrast to the Whedon version where
Silas Stone is still alive at the end of the

00:09:03.459 --> 00:09:04.800
movie.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:09.029
Another character who gets more development
in the Snyder Cut is Barry Allen, with this

00:09:09.029 --> 00:09:12.660
version setting up the Flash's upcoming movie
much better.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:17.209
Barry saved the world when he reversed time
allowing Cyborg and Superman to separate the

00:09:17.209 --> 00:09:19.600
boxes before they formed the Unity.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:24.519
We also saw him reverse time briefly during
the Superman resurrection scene when he didn't

00:09:24.519 --> 00:09:28.170
quite reach the Mother Box in time to charge
it initially.

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:32.160
This ability in the Snyder Cut together with
the first mention of the multiverse in the

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:37.820
DCEU movies so far, and the Joker discussing
alternate timelines with Batman in the epilogue,

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:41.959
set the stage for the Flashpoint movie in
a more satisfying way than the theatrical

00:09:41.959 --> 00:09:43.230
version did.

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:47.459
And although Barry is still the comic relief
among the group in both versions of the film,

00:09:47.459 --> 00:09:51.910
gone are some of the worst parts of Whedon's
reshoots such as Barry drawing on a prison

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:56.550
visitor's face, the tedious brunch jokes and,
of course, the moment where he falls on top

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:59.889
of Wonder Woman,
a tiresome joke that Whedon obviously finds

00:09:59.889 --> 00:10:03.910
so hilarious that he recycled it from Avengers:
Age of Ultron.

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:08.790
We also lost several other cringe-worthy Whedon
additions such as Martha telling Lois Lane

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:11.959
that Clark said she was "the thirstiest girl
he ever met"

00:10:11.959 --> 00:10:16.240
and Aquaman calling Wonder Woman 'gorgeous'
while under the influence of the Lasso of

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:17.240
Truth.

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:20.399
And there's an interesting technical fix to
the scene where the Justice League break into

00:10:20.399 --> 00:10:21.430
STAR Labs.

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:26.170
In the theatrical release, the date of birth
of Barry's fake military profile is November

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:31.019
2010, which at the time of the film's release,
2017, would have made the character only 7

00:10:31.019 --> 00:10:32.019
years old.

00:10:32.019 --> 00:10:37.300
In the Snyder Cut, the date has been fixed
so that the birth year is now 1997.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:41.499
Also much improved and fixed in Snyder's Justice
League are the villains.

00:10:41.499 --> 00:10:45.389
Steppenwolf's design has been vastly upgraded
together with his backstory and motivation

00:10:45.389 --> 00:10:46.740
for collecting the Mother Boxes.

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:51.399
It was a really intriguing added detail for
his armour to change with his mood and even

00:10:51.399 --> 00:10:56.170
retract entirely when he prostrates himself
in front of Darkseid.

00:10:56.170 --> 00:11:00.100
Steppenwolf is still a big CGI villain in
both versions, but his character is much better

00:11:00.100 --> 00:11:03.369
developed in the Snyder Cut,
which makes the final battle with him and

00:11:03.369 --> 00:11:07.610
the way the Justice League defeat him much
more satisfying than in the theatrical version.

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:13.000
But on top of that, the Snyder Cut also properly
reveals the real big bad of the movie and

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:18.120
of the Snyderverse as a whole, Darkseid, who
was referred to but never properly explained

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:19.440
in the Whedon cut.

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:22.990
In the Snyder Cut,
Darkseid also replaced Steppenwolf during

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:27.540
the extended and much more satisfying flashback
to the invasion of Earth when the Amazons,

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:33.209
Atlanteans, Gods, Green Lanterns and men successfully
resisted and repelled the villain and his

00:11:33.209 --> 00:11:34.209
minions.

00:11:34.209 --> 00:11:38.839
Something that puzzled many fans at the end
of the theatrical release is how Lex Luthor

00:11:38.839 --> 00:11:41.110
managed to escape from Arkham Asylum.

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:45.860
The Snyder Cut doesn't fully explain this
but does give a hint in the much longer epilogue

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:48.300
scene with Luthor and Deathstroke on the boat.

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:50.930
"I heard you were a few clowns short of a
circus."

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:55.509
"I was, thank you, until the good doctors
at Arkham helped me find some much needed

00:11:55.509 --> 00:11:56.509
clarity."

00:11:56.509 --> 00:12:00.119
This clown reference may hint that the Joker
was involved in Luthor's prison break

00:12:00.119 --> 00:12:04.389
and the mention of doctors here might also
suggest that Luthor bribed some of the staff

00:12:04.389 --> 00:12:09.519
at the facility to send over a bald lookalike
into his cell while he escaped.

00:12:09.519 --> 00:12:14.160
Another unanswered question from the theatrical
cut some fans have wondered about is how Aquaman

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:16.519
knew about the Atlantean Mother Box.

00:12:16.519 --> 00:12:20.759
The Snyder Cut's restoration of a deleted
scene between Arthur and Vulko helps clear

00:12:20.759 --> 00:12:23.439
this up:
"The snatchers have come from the dark place.

00:12:23.439 --> 00:12:24.899
They're looking for it.

00:12:24.899 --> 00:12:27.470
The Mother Box our people guard is not safe.

00:12:27.470 --> 00:12:29.699
Go to the stronghold of Atlantis."

00:12:29.699 --> 00:12:34.009
Likewise for Wonder Woman, the inclusion of
a deleted scene where Diana visits the Shrine

00:12:34.009 --> 00:12:39.069
of the Amazons explains how she learned about
Darkseid's previous invasion of Earth.

00:12:39.069 --> 00:12:43.589
The Snyder Cut isn't entirely faultless though
and there are a few inconsistencies that this

00:12:43.589 --> 00:12:48.170
version of the film seems to have introduced
in particular how it relates to the Aquaman

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:49.170
movie.

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:52.649
For example, Mera has a very strange British
accent which is completely at odds with the

00:12:52.649 --> 00:12:55.649
American accent she had in the Whedon cut
and the Aquaman film.

00:12:55.649 --> 00:12:58.040
"Where the legends say Atlan's trident was
forged."

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:00.800
"He's as shortsighted as he is cruel."

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:04.939
And then there's the way Aquaman tosses his
shirts into the ocean and smashes whiskey

00:13:04.939 --> 00:13:09.579
bottles by the shore but complains about rubbish
being thrown into the sea in his own movie.

00:13:09.579 --> 00:13:14.509
Now, did you spot other problems that the
Snyder Cut fixes or any new plot holes in

00:13:14.509 --> 00:13:16.110
this version of the movie?

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:19.519
Comment below and if you enjoyed this video
do leave a thumbs-up.

00:13:19.519 --> 00:13:23.439
Tap here to check out some more videos you
might like including the deleted alternate

00:13:23.439 --> 00:13:26.639
ending and cut scenes for The Boys Season
2.

00:13:26.639 --> 00:13:28.829
Thanks for watching and see ya next time!

00:13:28.829 --> 00:13:29.800
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

